theory M9c_InProcessSandbox
/*
  Model 9c - desktop-mediated login through a JS runtime embedded IN the core's
  own address space (QuickJS/Boa as a library, or a JS engine compiled to WASM
  under an in-process sandbox), rather than through a separate browser engine.

  This exists because M9b does not answer the question people actually ask.
  M9b's falsification turns on one step: the credential is handed to the engine
  over an adversary-observable channel (`Out(cred)`) because the engine is a
  separate process with a DevTools port, a /proc entry and an X11 surface. An
  in-process interpreter is not that object. There is no channel: the credential
  moves by function call inside the address space that already holds it. So
  M9b's proof does not transfer, and the honest thing is to model the design
  rather than to argue by analogy with a different one.

  The in-process design does win the thing it claims. What it buys is priced in
  a currency M9b never had to name.

  THE EDGE. To log in to a JavaScript site you must run the site's JavaScript,
  which is code the adversary chose. M9b ran it in a separate process, so an
  escape yielded whatever that process held - the credential in flight, bounded
  by the working set, which is the M6 floor the ladder already lives with. Here
  the interpreter shares an address space with the vault and the key that
  unseals it. An escape does not yield the item being used; it yields the store.
  Items never logged into, items for sites the user has not visited in years,
  and the master key itself.

  So the two designs get the same top-level verdict for opposite reasons, and
  the comparison that matters is not "does the credential leak" but "what is
  reachable when it does". `unused_credentials_stay_secret` is the lemma that
  separates them: it holds in M9b and falsifies here.

  A note on the obvious repair. Putting the interpreter back in its own process
  to contain an escape reintroduces the observable channel - that is M9b, by
  construction. The isolation that makes the runtime safe is the same isolation
  that makes it a domain member. This model does not prove that tension; it is
  visible from the two theories side by side.

  Expected:
    credential_never_leaks              FALSIFIED (all-traces) - same verdict as
        M9b, different mechanism: not a channel, an escape.
    credential_never_leaks_without_escape VERIFIED - the design's real claim,
        and one M9b cannot make at all: absent an escape, no credential reaches
        the domain. This is what an in-process runtime buys.
    unused_credentials_stay_secret      FALSIFIED (all-traces) - THE EDGE. In
        M9b an item the engine never handled stays secret. Here it does not.
    escape_takes_credentials_never_used (exists-trace) VERIFIED - witness for
        the above: the adversary derives an item that was never logged into.
    escape_takes_the_master_key         (exists-trace) VERIFIED - the second
        half of the address-space edge. M9b's engine never held this.
    login_succeeds                      (exists-trace) VERIFIED - sanity: the
        design does work when nobody attacks it.
*/
begin

// ---- The core, its key, and a vault of several items -------------------------

// One core process, holding the key that unseals the store.
rule Setup_Core:
    [ Fr(~master) ]
  --[ CoreStarted(~master), MasterKey(~master) ]->
    [ !Core(~master) ]

restriction OneCore:
  "All m1 m2 #i #j. CoreStarted(m1) @ i & CoreStarted(m2) @ j ==> #i = #j"

// Items in that one vault. Fires repeatedly: the point of this model is what
// happens to the items the login never touched, so there have to be some.
rule Add_Credential:
    [ !Core(master), Fr(~cred) ]
  --[ Secret(~cred, $O) ]->
    [ !Vault(~cred, $O), !SiteStore(~cred, $O) ]

// ---- The login ---------------------------------------------------------------

// The page's script. Adversary-chosen, because that is what "run the site's
// JavaScript" means: the code is supplied by whoever controls the page, and a
// site that has been compromised (or an origin the user was steered to) is
// exactly the case the sandbox is supposed to survive.
rule Load_Script:
    [ In(script) ]
  --[ ScriptLoaded(script) ]->
    [ !Script(script) ]

rule Human_Approve_Login:
    [ !Vault(cred, $O), Fr(~g) ]
  --[ LoginApproved(cred, $O) ]->
    [ LoginGrant(cred, $O) ]

// The credential enters the interpreter. Note what is NOT here: no `Out(cred)`.
// That single absence is the whole difference from `Engine_Login` in
// m9b_engine_login.spthy, and it is why the secrecy claim below survives in the
// no-escape case. Nothing is handed across a process boundary, so there is no
// DevTools port to attach to and no /proc/PID/mem to read.
rule Sandbox_Login:
    [ !Vault(cred, $O), LoginGrant(cred, $O), !Script(script),
      In(<'login', $O, tok>) ]
  --[ SandboxSawCred(cred, $O), LoginPerformed(cred, $O, tok) ]->
    [ CredToSite(cred, $O, tok), !Ran(script) ]

rule Site_CheckAndIssue:
    [ !SiteStore(cred, O), CredToSite(cred, O, tok), Fr(~sid) ]
  --[ SessionIssued(O, ~sid, tok), LoginOk(cred, O) ]->
    [ Out(<'session', O, ~sid>), StSite(O, ~sid, tok) ]

rule Site_Use_Session:
    [ StSite(O, sid, tok), In(<'use', O, sid>) ]
  --[ SessionUsed(O, sid) ]->
    [ ]

// ---- The escape --------------------------------------------------------------

// A sandbox escape, available once the hostile script has actually run.
//
// Modelled as reachable rather than as a possibility to be argued about: JS
// engines ship escapes on a schedule, and this one is interpreting code chosen
// by the adversary. The question a model can answer is not whether an escape
// happens but what it is worth, and that is the next three rules.
rule Sandbox_Escape:
    [ !Ran(script), In(<'escape', script>) ]
  --[ Escaped(script) ]->
    [ !Escaped(script) ]

// The edge, in one rule. The interpreter is inside the process that holds the
// store, so an escape reads the store - not the item in flight, every item.
rule Escape_Reads_Vault:
    [ !Escaped(script), !Vault(cred, O) ]
  --[ Exfiltrated(cred, O) ]->
    [ Out(cred) ]

// And the key, which no engine in M9b ever held.
rule Escape_Reads_Master_Key:
    [ !Escaped(script), !Core(master) ]
  --[ ExfiltratedMaster(master) ]->
    [ Out(master) ]

// ---- What the design claims --------------------------------------------------

// The headline, for comparability with M9a/M9b. Falsifies here, as in M9b, but
// by a different route.
lemma credential_never_leaks:
  "All c O #i.
     Secret(c, O) @ i ==> not(Ex #j. K(c) @ j)"

// The claim the in-process design actually makes, and the one M9b cannot make
// at all: absent an escape, nothing reaches the domain. There is no channel to
// watch, so watching gains nothing.
lemma credential_never_leaks_without_escape:
  "All c O #i.
     Secret(c, O) @ i
     ==> (Ex s #e. Escaped(s) @ e) | not(Ex #j. K(c) @ j)"

// ---- What it costs -----------------------------------------------------------

// THE EDGE, stated as a property. In M9b this holds: the engine only ever saw
// the credentials it was asked to submit, so an item never logged into is an
// item the adversary never gets. Here it falsifies, because the blast radius is
// the vault rather than the working set.
lemma unused_credentials_stay_secret:
  "All c O #i.
     Secret(c, O) @ i & not(Ex O2 #u. SandboxSawCred(c, O2) @ u)
     ==> not(Ex #j. K(c) @ j)"

// The witness, so the falsification above is not an artefact of how the lemma
// is phrased: a trace exists in which the adversary holds an item that was
// never used for a login.
lemma escape_takes_credentials_never_used:
  exists-trace
  "Ex c O #i #j.
     Exfiltrated(c, O) @ i & K(c) @ j
     & not(Ex O2 #u. SandboxSawCred(c, O2) @ u)"

// The second half of the address-space edge. An engine in another process can
// leak what it was given; it cannot leak the key that unseals everything else,
// because it never had it.
lemma escape_takes_the_master_key:
  exists-trace
  "Ex m #i #j. ExfiltratedMaster(m) @ i & K(m) @ j"

// ---- Sanity ------------------------------------------------------------------

lemma login_succeeds:
  exists-trace
  "Ex c O tok #i. LoginPerformed(c, O, tok) @ i"

end
